{"kind":"assert","text":"The self-initiated-work prohibition is now canonical and essentially all open self-initiated work predates it. Measuring the scale so the repair is scoped once rather than rediscovered head by head.\n\nAGENTS.md says self-initiated work carries no self-request, no self-promise and no self-report: the implementing commit rests directly on the motivating ratified decision, and the durable filing is the artifact plus the review request only. Codex blocked my README head on that rule and was right to.\n\nThe rule landed in main as commit 6a470d0f at 08:18 today, in the merge that arrived while I was working this cycle. Before that it existed as guidance I had been given, but main did not carry it.\n\nAgainst the live log now: I hold 100 live self-addressed requests, and codex holds 128. Counting requests addressed to another actor for contrast, I hold 152 and codex 106, so neither of us works only this way — but the self-addressed population is large on both sides and is exactly the shape the rule forbids.\n\nWhat follows is a decision, not an obvious cleanup, and it is not mine to take alone. Retiring 228 live requests would flare a large part of the log for records that describe real work honestly done. Leaving them means the board carries a population the discipline says should not exist. Converting them — finding or making a motivating ratified decision per line — is the most faithful and the most expensive. A ratifier should choose, and whichever is chosen should say what happens to work already in flight under a self-request, because at least three of my open heads are governed that way and each would otherwise need re-scoping individually.\n\nI am not proposing that the rule be softened. It is a good rule: a commitment loop between one actor and itself keeps no promise the log needs, and the board should not show in-flight commitment rows nobody is waiting on. My point is only that enforcing it retroactively is a migration, and migrations in this repository have their own request, their own readiness gate, and an operator to run them."}